WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.189
In a historic district in 
Pasadena, California,

00:00:03.189 --> 00:00:05.629
boundedby a Domino’s Pizza on one corner

00:00:05.629 --> 00:00:07.029
and a 7-11 on the other

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:08.115
you’ll find heaven.

00:00:08.115 --> 00:00:09.511
Bungalow Heaven.

00:00:13.816 --> 00:00:16.180
This type of house, the bungalow

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.510
and particularly the Craftsman bungalow

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:21.150
is now celebrated in historic districts from

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:24.635
Raleigh, North Carolina to 
Seattle, Washington.

00:00:24.635 --> 00:00:27.740
But the Craftsman bungalow 
only became a phenomenon

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.869
through the evolution of a 
socialist artist’s philosophy

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:35.339
to a mass market factory operation.

00:00:37.123 --> 00:00:40.199
The definition of a Craftsman bungalow 
is really slippery

00:00:40.199 --> 00:00:44.044
but to explain it, let’s take 
three 1911 examples.

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:49.576
From the American founder of the 
Craftsmanmovement, Gustav Stickley.

00:00:49.576 --> 00:00:52.629
These bungalows are all low, and wide,

00:00:52.629 --> 00:00:55.000
with roofs overhanging a porch.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.749
Compare that to a Queen Anne Victorian house,
common in the late 1800s.

00:00:59.749 --> 00:01:04.603
The Queen Anne has turrets and a wraparound
porch and all sorts of ornamentation.

00:01:04.603 --> 00:01:07.657
It was also huge and expensive.

00:01:07.657 --> 00:01:09.680
But bungalows aren’t just small —

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.130
you can see the difference when you compare
the shape to a New Orleans shotgun house,

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:18.394
which is small and low, but also 
long and skinny.

00:01:18.394 --> 00:01:22.775
The bungalow interiors feature 
relatively open floor plans like this:

00:01:22.775 --> 00:01:26.254
no long hallways, just connected spaces.

00:01:26.254 --> 00:01:29.252
And materials are hopefully natural:

00:01:29.252 --> 00:01:33.224
highlighting simple wood and stone 
over artifice.

00:01:33.224 --> 00:01:36.518
An ideal Craftsman interior 
might look like this:

00:01:36.518 --> 00:01:40.450
with built-in features like 
this window seat and piano,

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.533
beams showing off the materials,

00:01:42.533 --> 00:01:46.815
and details like these hanging lanterns
that hang from the ceiling.

00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:50.740
A true Craftsman bungalow is simple, 
but well-appointed.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:54.027
Built carefully, with attention to every detail.

00:01:54.027 --> 00:01:57.364
And that highlights the 
Craftsman bungalow paradox.

00:01:57.364 --> 00:02:02.640
How did a meticulously crafted movement 
become a mass movement?

00:02:05.452 --> 00:02:07.781
I’ll be honest, before I started this video

00:02:07.781 --> 00:02:11.910
I kinda thought Craftsman tools 
is where Craftsman came from.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.080
And it is true that they are kinda connected

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.729
but it started somewhere else entirely.

00:02:19.347 --> 00:02:24.195
These chairs are from the Crystal palace exhibition
of 1851, in London.

00:02:24.195 --> 00:02:29.790
It was a massive exhibition showing off 
the newest, most advanced...everything.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.672
The arts and crafts movement was born in response:

00:02:32.672 --> 00:02:37.050
a push to stop making these chairs 
and start making ones like these.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:39.050
Cleaner. Simpler.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:44.470
Poet slash artist slash textile guy 
slash socialist William Morris

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:46.762
was one of many Arts and Crafts figures.

00:02:46.762 --> 00:02:50.930
But he became key to the Craftsman movement,
in America, after his death.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:53.356
Today, he’s best known for his gorgeous designs.

00:02:53.356 --> 00:02:57.415
Like on these playing cards I’ve got that
were made in some factory in China.

00:02:57.415 --> 00:03:01.660
And you can get stuff with his original designs
printed on it almost anywhere

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:06.425
from playing cards to your quaint,
pandemic face masks.

00:03:06.425 --> 00:03:09.950
Morris reacted against industrialization 
and artifice

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:12.520
that was happening during the industrial revolution.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.990
He was in favor of craftsmanship and beauty.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:16.570
He inspired Gustav Stickley--

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:20.300
the guy who wrote that book of Craftsman plans
and made that chair--

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:23.990
to promote the Arts and Crafts movement 
in America

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:28.820
with his 1900s magazine, The Craftsman.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:33.830
The first issue was all about William Morris,
including his Socialistic career

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:36.220
which united with his art, in that a workman

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:41.085
could pursue the “creation of beauty
as necessary as daily bread.”

00:03:41.085 --> 00:03:45.010
Craftsmen, he claimed, found value 
in the quality of their work

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:48.014
not as disengaged factory employees.

00:03:48.014 --> 00:03:52.350
Future editions of “The Craftsman” promoted
similarly eclectic topics,

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:55.000
from poets to Factory reform.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.840
And there was a lot of furniture,
since that was Stickley’s background.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:03.903
The magazine’s name, Craftsman, became the
way to identify Arts and Crafts in America.

00:04:03.903 --> 00:04:09.316
Entire Craftsman homes started to show up
in the magazine as well, including, in 1903,

00:04:09.316 --> 00:04:11.322
the Crafstman bungalow.

00:04:14.755 --> 00:04:16.790
“How to build a bungalow”

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:19.700
noted that the word started 
"near the banks of the ganges"

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:24.140
but had since been transformed 
to a new architectural form.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:28.230
The Craftsman had recommendations 
for how to build it all properly.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.671
Over time, the magazine featured 
more and more Craftsman house plans

00:04:33.671 --> 00:04:36.017
including plans for bungalows.

00:04:36.017 --> 00:04:40.587
Adhering to those Arts and Crafts tenets:
natural materials,

00:04:40.587 --> 00:04:42.790
intentional construction and

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:47.619
as bungalows, a low slung shape and relatively
open floor plan.

00:04:47.619 --> 00:04:50.993
Stickley tied the bungalow 
to the Craftsman movement.

00:04:50.993 --> 00:04:55.529
And then dollar signs showed up on the plans.

00:04:55.529 --> 00:04:58.460
Craftsman and bungalow had become 
these buzz words

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:00.314
kinda like “Tiny House” today.

00:05:00.314 --> 00:05:02.430
And nobody controlled that brand.

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:05.385
So all the bungalows started getting 
lumped together.

00:05:05.385 --> 00:05:09.090
I love these two papers — by Janet Ore and
Kim Hernandez

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:13.666
that show how bungalows got commercialized
in the “development of the Seattle Suburbs”

00:05:13.666 --> 00:05:16.534
and in a Los Angeles “Bungalow Boom”.

00:05:18.047 --> 00:05:22.802
Across the country in the 1900s,
new areas needed hundreds of homes.

00:05:22.802 --> 00:05:26.300
Stickley himself started selling floor plans
so Craftsman fans

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:29.246
could build their own homes 
with their own builders,

00:05:29.246 --> 00:05:31.750
including craftsman bungalows.

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:35.510
Originally, Craftsman homes 
came from Stickley’s plans

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:37.927
or a few other esteemed practitioners.

00:05:37.927 --> 00:05:43.325
But anybody could sell floor plans or build
houses and make a lot of money off that style.

00:05:43.325 --> 00:05:46.420
In Seattle, an entrepreneur 
named Jud Yoho—

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:48.389
Jud Yoho.

00:05:48.389 --> 00:05:50.154
Do not trust Jud Yoho.

00:05:51.609 --> 00:05:54.428
Jud Yoho sold “Craftsman bungalows” 

00:05:54.428 --> 00:05:57.324
with no real affiliation to the 
Arts and Crafts movement

00:05:57.324 --> 00:05:59.128
 and the “creation of beauty.”

00:05:59.128 --> 00:06:03.430
His goal was to turn “craftsman” 
into a volume business.

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:05.590
The same thing happened down in Los Angeles

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:09.520
where “Practical Bungalows” were built
by the Los Angeles Investment Company

00:06:11.343 --> 00:06:15.180
a real estate firm that developed land, 
took Craftsman style

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:19.655
and built tons of homes with the help 
of a massive mill and shops.

00:06:19.655 --> 00:06:23.700
Sears — then a catalog company — was the
Amazon of the era.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:25.110
And they joined in.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:29.514
They sold 70,000 kit homes 
between 1908 and 1940

00:06:29.514 --> 00:06:33.430
 including a bunch of bungalows 
with Craftsman flair.

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:37.580
Salesman from Portland to Topeka 
packaged bungalow style,

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:42.230
all of which had a similar floorplan and,
often, a Craftsman-like style

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:46.500
but without Gustav Stickley or the philosophy
of the Arts and Crafts movement.

00:06:47.121 --> 00:06:51.389
By 1909, the New York Times was publishing
full blown trend pieces

00:06:51.389 --> 00:06:53.460
about the “call of the bungalow”

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:58.490
it was the “Latest Dream of Spring Poet
and Real Estate Man.”

00:06:59.421 --> 00:07:02.899
By the 1920s, “Craftsman” 
was such a strong brand 

00:07:02.899 --> 00:07:06.560
that Sears bought the Trademark in 1927 

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:08.560
and they put it on tools.

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:16.090
What started as a movement for a socialist
textile maker had become a goldrush

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:20.935
where different types of bungalows capitalized
on the “Craftsman” cachet.

00:07:20.935 --> 00:07:26.751
A craftsman bungalow can look like 
an artistic expression of individual mastery.

00:07:26.751 --> 00:07:31.300
Or it can look like hustlers 
capitalizing on a trend.

00:07:31.804 --> 00:07:35.585
But really, it can look like 
both of those things.

00:07:35.585 --> 00:07:39.550
And that, to a lot of people,

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:40.834
looks just like heaven.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:55.770
Okay so there is a whole world of people

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:59.180
who are obsessed with these kit homes and
where they actually ended up.

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:01.490
And a lot of those include Craftsman homes.

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:05.820
I found one blog called 
"Oklahoma Houses By Mail"

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:09.290
and this woman had found the 
Los Angeles Investment Company catalog

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:11.313
that I show earlier in this video.

00:08:11.313 --> 00:08:14.570
And then she tracked down the location of
the house that is on the cover.

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:16.760
And you can find it on streetview today.

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:19.660
It's got a different coat of paint
but it is the same building.

